course: physics
lesson: 17
title: What sound is: wobbles in the air
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every sound starts with something wobbling very fast: a guitar string, a drum skin, or the vocal cords in your throat.
explain: The wobbling thing shoves the air next to it, that air shoves the next bit, and the wobble travels outwards until it reaches your ear, a bit like a push passed along a queue.
explain: Put your hand on your throat and hum, and you can feel your voice buzzing; stop the wobble, and the sound stops too.
explain: Fast wobbles sound high and slow wobbles sound low, so a tiny ukulele squeaks and a huge double bass booms.
explain: Sound needs something to travel through, air, water or solid things, so in the emptiness of space there is no sound at all.
fact: Sound is a wobble, or vibration, passed on through air, water or solid things.
fact: A plucked guitar string wobbles back and forth very fast and pushes the air in little waves; press a finger on it and the sound stops.
fact: When you hum, your vocal cords wobble as air from your lungs passes them, and you can feel the buzz on your throat.
fact: Rice on a drum jumps when you bang it, because the drum skin wobbles up and down; hit gently and the rice barely moves and the sound is quiet.
fact: Short, thin, tight strings wobble fast and sound high; long, thick, slack strings wobble slowly and sound low; blowing across bottles with different amounts of water gives different notes, because the air inside wobbles at different speeds.
fact: An echo is sound bouncing back off a hard, flat surface such as a cliff; the longer the echo takes to come back, the further away the cliff is.
fact: Hard bare walls bounce sound back, while soft curtains, carpets and sofas soak it up, so a furnished room hardly echoes.
fact: A bat finds its way in the dark by making very high squeaks and listening for the echoes.
fact: Ships use echoes under water, called sonar, and doctors use very high sounds, called ultrasound, to see inside the body.
fact: Dogs can hear much higher notes than people can, and people hear the highest notes less and less as they grow older.
fact: Sound travels further and faster in water than in air, and whale songs can carry hundreds of kilometres.
fact: In empty space there is nothing to carry the wobble, so no sound can travel; real astronauts talk to each other by radio.
exercise: What is sound? | A wobble passed on through air, water or solids | A kind of light that you hear | Tiny bits flying out of the thing that made it | Air that has become heavier
exercise: Why does a guitar string make a note when you pluck it? | It wobbles fast and pushes the air in little waves | It rubs against the wooden neck and squeaks like a door | It lets out air that was stored inside it | It gets hot and buzzes like a light bulb
exercise: What happens if you press your finger on a ringing guitar string? | The wobble stops, and so does the sound | It gets much louder and keeps on ringing | It makes a higher note for ever | Nothing changes at all
exercise: What do you feel on your throat when you hum? | Your vocal cords buzzing | Your heart beating faster | Air going into your stomach | Nothing at all
exercise: Why does rice jump on a drum when you bang it? | The drum skin wobbles up and down and throws it | The rice is scared by the noise | The air trapped inside the drum blows it up into the air | The drum gets hot and the rice pops
exercise: Why does a big double bass make lower notes than a tiny guitar? | Its long, thick strings wobble more slowly | Its strings wobble much faster than a guitar's | It is made of a darker wood | It is played by bigger people
exercise: What is an echo? | Sound bouncing back off a hard surface | A second sound made by the cliff itself | Sound getting stuck in the air | Your ears repeating a sound
exercise: Why does an empty room echo but a furnished room does not? | Soft things soak up the sound instead of bouncing it | Furniture makes its own sounds that cancel the echo | Empty rooms are always colder | Sound cannot travel past furniture at all
exercise: How does a bat fly in the dark without bumping into things? | It listens for echoes of its high squeaks | It sees perfectly well in total darkness with its eyes | It smells every wall and tree | It flies only along paths it remembers
exercise: Why can a dog hear a whistle that you cannot? | Its ears pick up much higher notes than ours | The whistle is too quiet for anybody | Dogs hear through their noses | The whistle only works when you are not listening
exercise: Through what does sound travel better and further, water or air? | Water | Air | Both exactly the same | Sound cannot travel in water
exercise: Why can nobody hear you shout in space? | There is nothing there to carry the wobble | Space is far too cold for any sound to work | The stars are too noisy | Helmets block all sounds
exercise: How do astronauts talk to each other outside the space station? | By radio | By shouting very loudly | By tapping on their helmets | By whistling high notes
exercise: What do ships use to find the sea floor with echoes? | Sonar | A telescope | A compass | A thermometer
exercise: Why do older people often not hear very high notes? | Hearing of the highest notes fades as people get older | High notes are louder for grown-ups | Their ears grow and catch only low notes | Older people choose not to listen
exercise: If you shout at a cliff and count the time until the echo, what can you learn? | Roughly how far away the cliff is | How tall the cliff is from top to bottom | What kind of rock the cliff is made of | How many thousands of years old the cliff is
test: In a space film a spaceship explodes with a huge boom. What is wrong with that? | There is no air in space to carry sound | Nothing, explosions are always loud | Space makes sounds louder, not quieter | Only small explosions are silent in space
test: me mate reckons whales just splash about, they can't hear each other far. right? | No, their songs travel hundreds of kilometres under water | Yes, sound stops a few metres under water | Yes, whales are deaf | No, but only in warm seas
test: You blow across bottles with different amounts of water and hear different notes. Which idea does this show? | Different-sized wobbles make different notes | Water makes all sounds the same | Bottles only make a sound when they are completely empty | The water itself sings
test: What would happen to a guitar if its strings were made very slack and loose? | Its notes would sound lower | Its notes would sound higher | It would make no sound at all | It would sound exactly the same
test: In a cartoon, a character shouts and the words visibly fly through the air as letters. What really travels? | A wobble in the air | Tiny bits of the voice | Little puffs of light | The words themselves as invisible letters
test: Why do concert halls have special shapes and soft materials? | To control how sound bounces so the music sounds right | To keep the musicians warm | To stop the sound reaching the audience | To make the hall look bigger
test: shut it, you can't hear nothing under water, everyone knows that | Wrong, sound travels well under water, even better than in air | Right, water blocks all sound | Right, only fish can make sound | Wrong, but only for very loud bangs
test: Doctors want to see a baby inside its mum without any cuts. What do they use? | Ultrasound, very high sounds and their echoes | Loud music played into the tummy | A bright torch shone straight through the skin of the tummy | A magnet and a compass
test: Your new bedroom is empty and echoey. What would make it quieter? | Adding curtains, a rug and soft furniture | Painting all four walls a much darker colour | Opening all the windows | Taking out the light bulb
test: Which shows that you must have something wobbling to make a sound? | A drum stops sounding when you hold its skin still | Light shines without making any sound | A cold room is always quieter than a warm one with the heating on | Shadows make no noise at all
test: Your gran says she cannot hear the high beep of the oven any more but you can. Why? | Hearing of high notes fades with age | Her oven makes two different beeps | Children's ears are louder | The beep is too low for older people
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon of sound? | It is a vibration transmitted through a material medium | It is a form of light perceptible to the ear | It is a stream of particles emitted by the source | It is a property solely of the human ear
exam: Ascertain the reason for which sound cannot be transmitted through the vacuum of space. | There exists no medium to transmit the vibration | The temperature of space is excessively low for any vibration | The radiation of the stars obstructs it | Sound is absorbed by the light of the Sun
exam: It is given that a string is shortened and tightened. Determine the consequent alteration in its pitch. | The pitch is raised | The pitch is lowered | The pitch is unaltered | The string ceases to produce sound
exam: Identify the means by which a bat determines the position of obstacles in darkness. | By the reception of echoes of its own high-pitched calls | By vision unaffected by the absence of light | By the sense of smell alone | By memory of all previous flights
